The `sized_iter!` macro is a custom declarative macro (using `macro_rules!`) presented in the book *Hands-On Concurrency with Rust*. It is used to generate boilerplate code for signed integer iterators that iterate from a minimum value up to a maximum value.

### Usage and Definition
The macro takes three arguments: the name of the struct to create, the integer type, and the minimum value of that type.

```rust
macro_rules! sized_iter {
    ($name:ident, $int:ty, $min:expr) => {
        #[derive(Default)]
        pub struct $name {
            cur: $int,
            done: bool,
        }

        impl Iterator for $name {
            type Item = $int;

            fn next(&mut self) -> Option<$int> {
                if self.done {
                    return None;
                }
                let val = self.cur;
                self.cur += 1;
                if self.cur > $min { // Logic varies based on signed/unsigned context
                    self.done = true;
                }
                Some(val)
            }
        }
    };
}
```

### Examples
It is typically used to define iterators for various signed integer types like `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, etc.:

```rust
sized_iter!(SmallI8, i8, i8::min_value());
sized_iter!(SmallI16, i16, i16::min_value());
sized_iter!(SmallI32, i32, i32::min_value());
sized_iter!(SmallI64, i64, i64::min_value());
sized_iter!(SmallIsize, isize, isize::min_value());
```

### Context
In the source text, this macro is used alongside an `unsized_iter!` macro to reduce code duplication when creating iterators for testing purposes (specifically for verifying memory sizes and counts of different integer types).

