In Rust, making a struct public (using the `pub` keyword) **does not** automatically make its fields public. Even if a struct is marked as `pub`, its fields remain private by default and can only be accessed by modules within the same crate (or the same module, depending on the scope).

To allow external access to fields, you have two primary options:

### 1. Make the fields public
You can explicitly mark each field with the `pub` keyword. This makes the fields accessible to any module that can see the struct.

```rust
pub struct Product {
    pub id: u64,
    pub name: String,
    pub price: f64,
}
```

### 2. Use Getter and Setter methods
To maintain encapsulation (hiding the internal representation of the data while still allowing access), you can keep the fields private and provide public methods to interact with them.

```rust
pub struct Product {
    id: u64,         // Private
    name: String,    // Private
    price: f64,      // Private
}

impl Product {
    pub fn new(id: u64, name: String, price: f64) -> Self {
        Self { id, name, price }
    }

    pub fn id(&self) -> u64 {
        self.id
    }

    pub fn set_price(&mut self, price: f64) {
        self.price = price;
    }
}
```

